-- title:
--   Universal property of the modular function field K(̃ j,̃ j_N)
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field and $N\ge 1$ an integer with $N\neq 0$ in $K$. Inside the Laurent series field $K((q))$ put $\tilde j = \mathtt{jqModC}\,K = q^{-1}\cdot(\mathrm{jNum}\bmod K)$, the image under $\mathbb{Z}\to K$ of the integral power series $E_4^{3}\cdot\eta^{-24}$-type numerator $\mathtt{jNum}$ shifted by $q^{-1}$, and $\tilde j_N = \mathtt{jqNModC}\,K\,N$, its image under the ring homomorphism $\mathtt{qExpand}$ multiplying all exponents by $N$ (that is, $q\mapsto q^{N}$). Let $\mathtt{modularFunctionFieldC}\,K\,N = K(\tilde j,\tilde j_N)$ be the intermediate field of $K((q))/K$ generated by these two series. Let $\mathtt{data}$ consist of a polynomial $\Phi\in\mathbb{Z}[X][Y]$ that is monic in $Y$, of degree $\sum_{d\mid N,\ d\text{ squarefree}} N/d$, and satisfies $\Phi(j(q),j(q^{N}))=0$ for the rational $q$-expansion of $j$; assume moreover that the one-variable polynomial $\Phi(\tilde j_N,Y)$, obtained by evaluating each coefficient of $\Phi$ at $\tilde j_N$, is irreducible over the subfield $K(\tilde j_N)\subseteq K((q))$. Then for every field $A$ with a $K$-algebra structure and all $c,y\in A$ with $c$ transcendental over $K$ and $\Phi(c,y)=0$, there is exactly one $K$-algebra homomorphism $\psi\colon K(\tilde j,\tilde j_N)\to A$ with $\psi(\tilde j)=c$ and $\psi(\tilde j_N)=y$.
--
--   This is the universal property identifying the $q$-expansion model $K(\tilde j,\tilde j_N)\subseteq K((q))$ of the function field of $X_0(N)$ with $K(X)[Y]/(\Phi_N(X,Y))$, the irreducibility input being Igusa's theorem on modular equations. It is used to produce $K$-algebra isomorphisms of this field prescribed on the two generators, in particular the involution exchanging $\tilde j$ and $\tilde j_N$ and the comparison with a geometric model.
